backend-1   | │                                                                              │
backend-1   | │  Agent: Schema Analysis Specialist                                           │
backend-1   | │                                                                              │
backend-1   | │  Final Answer:                                                               │
backend-1   | │  **Answer**                                                                  │
backend-1   | │                                                                              │
backend-1   | │  Below is the precise set of ClickHouse tables, the join logic that          │
backend-1   | │  connects them, and the exact columns that must be selected to calculate     │
backend-1   | │  **total movie revenue** and **total number of views**, grouped by           │
backend-1   | │  **age‑rating** and **film‑genre**.                                          │
backend-1   | │                                                                              │
backend-1   | │  | Section | Details |                                                       │
backend-1   | │  |---------|---------|                                                       │
backend-1   | │  | **tables** | `dev_film`, `dev_film_category`, `dev_category`,             │
backend-1   | │  `dev_inventory`, `dev_rental`, `dev_payment` *(or the partitioned payment   │
backend-1   | │  tables such as `dev_payment_p2022_01` … `dev_payment_p2022_07` if the data  │
backend-1   | │  is stored only there)* |                                                    │
backend-1   | │  | **join_path** | 1. `dev_film` **←→** `dev_film_category` on               │
backend-1   | │  `dev_film.film_id = dev_film_category.film_id`  <br>2. `dev_film_category`  │
backend-1   | │  **←→** `dev_category` on `dev_film_category.category_id =                   │
backend-1   | │  dev_category.category_id`  <br>3. `dev_film` **←→** `dev_inventory` on      │
backend-1   | │  `dev_film.film_id = dev_inventory.film_id`  <br>4. `dev_inventory` **←→**   │
backend-1   | │  `dev_rental` on `dev_inventory.inventory_id = dev_rental.inventory_id`      │
backend-1   | │  <br>5. `dev_rental` **←→** `dev_payment` on `dev_rental.rental_id =         │
backend-1   | │  dev_payment.rental_id` |                                                    │
backend-1   | │  | **required_columns** | • **dev_film** – `film_id`, `rating` (age‑rating)  │
backend-1   | │  <br>• **dev_film_category** – `film_id`, `category_id` <br>•                │
backend-1   | │  **dev_category** – `category_id`, `name` (genre name) <br>•                 │
backend-1   | │  **dev_inventory** – `inventory_id`, `film_id` <br>• **dev_rental** –        │
backend-1   | │  `rental_id`, `inventory_id` <br>• **dev_payment** – `rental_id`, `amount`   │
backend-1   | │  (revenue) |                                                                 │
backend-1   | │  | **aggregation logic** | ```sql\nSELECT\n    f.rating          AS          │
backend-1   | │  age_rating,\n    c.name            AS genre,\n    SUM(p.amount)     AS      │
backend-1   | │  total_revenue,\n    COUNT(r.rental_id) AS total_views\nFROM dev_film AS     │
backend-1   | │  f\nJOIN dev_film_category AS fc ON f.film_id = fc.film_id\nJOIN             │
backend-1   | │  dev_category AS c      ON fc.category_id = c.category_id\nJOIN              │
backend-1   | │  dev_inventory AS i    ON f.film_id = i.film_id\nJOIN dev_rental AS r        │
backend-1   | │  ON i.inventory_id = r.inventory_id\nJOIN dev_payment AS p      ON           │
backend-1   | │  r.rental_id = p.rental_id\nGROUP BY f.rating, c.name;\n```<br>(If the       │
backend-1   | │  payment data lives in the monthly partitioned tables, replace               │
backend-1   | │  `dev_payment` with a `UNION ALL` of those tables, but the column list       │
backend-1   | │  stays the same.) |                                                          │
backend-1   | │                                                                              │
backend-1   | │  **Explanation**                                                             │
backend-1   | │                                                                              │
backend-1   | │  * **Age rating** is stored in `dev_film.rating`.                            │
backend-1   | │  * **Genre** (film classification) is a many‑to‑many relation via            │
backend-1   | │  `dev_film_category` → `dev_category.name`.                                  │
backend-1   | │  * **Revenue** comes from the `amount` column of the payment table, linked   │
backend-1   | │  back to a film through the chain `payment → rental → inventory → film`.     │
backend-1   | │  * **Views** are represented by the number of rental records (`dev_rental`)  │
backend-1   | │  that reference a given film.                                                │
backend-1   | │                                                                              │
backend-1   | │  Using the tables, joins, and columns listed above will yield the required   │
backend-1   | │  totals per age‑rating and genre without any ambiguity.                      │
backend-1   | │                                                                              │
